          "eyebrow": "Recap",
          "title": "The grid, addresses, and ranges",
          "lede": "Everything in a spreadsheet starts from the cell address and the range.",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "prose",
              "block_id": "tile_spreadsheet_grid_references.s09.b1",
              "role": "takeaway",
              "text": "A cell is found by its column-letter/row-number address, and a workbook can hold several grids as separate sheets. A reference points at one cell or a block of cells; a range like A10:E20 covers a rectangular block, while H:H covers an entire column. Every formula you write from here will use one of these reference forms.",
